Say who sent the message that nobody is reading.

The WATCHED flag answered its question and raised a better one. lsd holds a
message on port 0xf03 with no knote on it, and lsdiconservice holds one on
port 0xc03 the same way, so the ports that are stuck are not ports anybody
is listening to. That rules out the lost wakeup the previous round was built
around -- there is no wakeup to lose -- but it leaves two readings that call
for completely different work, and the output cannot tell them apart.

Either the message came from the process that is blocked waiting for its
answer, in which case the receiver has the right and simply is not listening
on it, and the fault is in how the listener is armed. Or it came from
somewhere else entirely, in which case this mail is unrelated and the
blocked caller's message is sitting somewhere this report has not looked.

The answer is already in the message. The trailer is filled at copyin with
the sending task's audit token, and its sixth word is the sender's pid, so a
queued message names the process that is waiting on it without any
bookkeeping of ours. Alongside it goes msgh_id, which identifies the routine
being asked for, and whether the port belongs to a port set -- a port
received on through a set carries no knote of its own, and without that
distinction an ordinary run-loop port is indistinguishable from an abandoned
one. The previous round's "unwatched" may have been exactly that mistake.

    port 0xf03 1 msg 3 senders unwatched from pid 57 id 0x1b5d

With a line like that, one boot settles which of the two it is.
